SUMMARY:Machine Learning in Epidemiology with R — Part II: From Trees to Neural Networks - R-Ladies Rome
DESCRIPTION:**How do different machine-learning models learn from the same data, and how can we compare their predictions?**\n\nJoin [R-Ladies Rome](https://rladiesrome.org/) for the second workshop in our **Machine Learning in Epidemiology with R** series, led by [Federica Gazzelloni](https://federicagazzelloni.com/).\n\nPart I introduced how models learn through loss and optimisation.\n\nPart II, we’ll put these ideas into practice by training and comparing decision trees, random forests and a simple neural network using a heart-disease classification example.\n\nWe’ll follow a complete workflow in R: explore the data, define a prediction task, train models and evaluate their performance on observations they have not seen during training.\n\n**What we’ll cover**\n\n* Decision trees and random forests with `mlr3`.\n* A simple neural network with Keras.\n* Layers, activation functions, epochs and batch sizes.\n* Training and validation loss.\n* Model comparison using accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and ROC/AUC.\n* Overfitting, cross-validation and the distinction between prediction and causation.\n\n**Who is this for?**\nThis workshop is for people interested in applying machine learning to epidemiology and health data. Basic familiarity with R and RStudio is recommended. The session builds on Part I and begins with a short recap.\n\n**What to bring**\nA computer with R and RStudio installed. Setup instructions and workshop materials will be shared before the session.\n\nBring your questions and join us for a practical exploration of how different models learn—and how we assess their predictions.
LOCATION:Online event
URL:https://www.meetup.com/rladies-rome/events/316945048/
ORGANIZER;CN=R-Ladies Rome:mailto:info@rladies.org
CATEGORIES:RLadies+,R-Ladies Rome
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SEQUENCE:0
